\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{pathfinder-note}

\title{Prediction Quality and Reward Accounting in Open-Team Learning}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\paragraph{The pair.}
Q surveys learning-augmented guarantees and explains why component results require explicit interface, cost, and benchmark assumptions before they support system-level conclusions.
P proposes MARS-RA, which converts multimodal pairwise comparisons into Bradley--Terry contribution scores and uses normalized scores for potential-based reward shaping in cooperative learning with changing agent participation.

\paragraph{The connexion pursued.}
The investigation initially considered a comparison-error-to-policy-return trade-off, but shifted to whether prediction-derived shaping preserves its objective through an open-team training interface.
Statistical consistency of a score estimator is different from Q's algorithmic consistency under exact predictions.
Moreover, coherent potential differences preserve payoff orderings independently of comparison accuracy.
The substantive connection is therefore Q's interface discipline applied to P's path from comparisons through active-agent scores to training returns.
This does not establish a learning-augmented performance classification for P.
\ledger{1, 6}
The original argument is recorded in
\url{ada/open_team_shaping.md}, lines 9--43.

This account preserves the preceding consolidation and appends the fifth-round response to the latest verifier review, entry 46.
Reviews 8, 17, and 28 prompted the implementation audit, accounting experiments, neural exploration diagnostic, and approximate-critic analysis retained below.
Entry 46 accepted their qualified findings but judged the publication case insufficient.
The appended round tests transfer to an ordinary public MAPPO trainer and a standard cooperative environment.
Earlier assessments and proposed next steps are retained as historical stages.
Entries 59--60 report completion and self-assessment, not independent acceptance.
The material remains thin for a publication-level claim.
\ledger{8, 17, 28, 44, 46, 58, 59, 60}

\paragraph{Objective invariance precedes prediction accuracy.}
Fix permanent agent identities, a finite horizon \(T\), a discount factor \(\gamma\), and a prediction-generating protocol.
Let \(\Phi_t^i\) be a bounded, pre-action potential, possibly random and history dependent.
Require the same realized potential at the shared endpoint of adjacent transitions, terminal potential \(\Phi_T^i=0\), and an initial potential distribution independent of the policy being compared.
If every global transition contributes to the return, then
\[
\begin{aligned}
F_t^i &= \gamma\Phi_{t+1}^i-\Phi_t^i,\\
\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^t F_t^i
&= -\Phi_0^i.
\end{aligned}
\]
For fixed shaping weight \(\rho\), this gives
\[
J_i^{\mathrm{sh}}(\pi)
=J_i(\pi)-\rho\,\mathbb E[\Phi_0^i].
\]
Payoff orderings, best responses, and Nash equilibria are unchanged; with shared original payoffs, team maximizers are unchanged as well.
This is established potential-based reward-shaping theory, not a new result.
It neither proves convergence of MAPPO nor explains finite-training improvements.
Comparison quality can still affect learning dynamics and computational cost, but P's estimator theorem does not supply that bridge.
\ledger{1, 4, 6}
See \url{ada/open_team_shaping.md}, lines 21--43.

\paragraph{The conditional open-team accounting defect.}
P estimates an active-set score vector and subtracts successive softmax potentials without specifying permanent-coordinate alignment when participation changes.
A fixed-population embedding resolves the dimensional problem, but does not by itself resolve reward masking.
\ledger{2}
See \url{ada/open_team_shaping.md}, lines 13--19 and 47--60.

Let \(m_t^i\in\{0,1\}\) indicate whether the shaping reward on transition \(t\) is retained for agent \(i\).
Reindexing the positive potential terms yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^t m_t^i F_t^i
={}&-m_0^i\Phi_0^i\\
&+\sum_{t=1}^{T-1}
  \gamma^t(m_{t-1}^i-m_t^i)\Phi_t^i\\
&+\gamma^T m_{T-1}^i\Phi_T^i.
\end{aligned}
\]
With terminal-zero potentials and zero inactive coordinates, an exit contributes zero to the interior sum.
Each reentry contributes a negative discounted entry potential.
If entry times or potentials depend on earlier actions, this residual can change policy preferences even when the intended potentials are supplied exactly.
\ledger{2, 5}

For the recorded counterexample, take \(T=4\), \(\gamma=9/10\), and \(\rho=1\).
An initial choice selects Stay or Recharge:
\[
\begin{aligned}
m_{\mathrm{S}}&=(1,1,1,1),\\
\Phi_{\mathrm{S}}&=(1/2,1/2,1/2,1/2,0),\\
m_{\mathrm{R}}&=(1,0,1,1),\\
\Phi_{\mathrm{R}}&=(1/2,0,1/2,1/2,0).
\end{aligned}
\]
Stay has base return zero; Recharge receives \(1/10\) on the initial transition and zero thereafter.
Full-time shaping totals \(-1/2\) on both paths.
Masked shaping instead totals
\[
S_{\mathrm{S}}=-\frac12,
\qquad
S_{\mathrm{R}}=-\frac{181}{200}.
\]
Consequently, the masked shaped returns are
\[
J_{\mathrm{S}}^{\mathrm{sh}}=-\frac12,
\qquad
J_{\mathrm{R}}^{\mathrm{sh}}=-\frac{161}{200},
\]
reversing the base preference for Recharge.
The additional charge is \(-81/200\).
Thus \(-181/200\) is the shaping return and \(-161/200\) includes the environmental reward.
This is an accounting counterexample, not a failure observed in P's code.
\ledger{5, 6, 8, 17}

The rational-arithmetic checker verifies the identity on \(3{,}888\) mask/potential assignments and checks the counterexample and its repairs.
The construction and verification record are in
\url{ada/open_team_shaping.md}, lines 62--71 and 154--156;
the executable and preserved output are
\url{ada/check_shaping.py} and
\url{ada/check_shaping_01.log}.
This is a mathematical toy verification, not a MARS-RA replication.
\ledger{5}

\paragraph{A conditional return-loss bound and repairs.}
Express the implemented scalar shaping term as
\[
\begin{aligned}
\widehat F_t
&=\gamma\Phi_{t+1}-\Phi_t+e_t,\\
D_\pi
&=\mathbb E_\pi\!\left[
  \sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^t e_t
  \right].
\end{aligned}
\]
For a declared common objective or scalarization, fixed predictor protocol, policy-independent initial potential mean \(C\), terminal-zero potential, and \(\rho\geq0\),
\[
\widehat J(\pi)=J(\pi)-\rho C+\rho D_\pi.
\]
Suppose \(\widehat\pi\) is \(\xi\)-optimal for this implemented objective over a declared policy class, and \(\pi^\star\) maximizes its base objective.
Substitution into shaped optimality gives
\[
\begin{aligned}
J(\pi^\star)-J(\widehat\pi)
&\leq \xi+\rho(D_{\widehat\pi}-D_{\pi^\star})\\
&\leq \xi+\rho\left(
  \sup_\pi D_\pi-\inf_\pi D_\pi
  \right).
\end{aligned}
\]
A uniform bound \(\lvert D_\pi\rvert\leq B\) therefore gives loss at most \(\xi+2\rho B\).
This elementary perturbation argument separates optimization error from accounting error.
It does not establish shaped optimization accuracy for neural MAPPO, and a logged trajectory does not establish a uniform policy bound.
\ledger{5, 6}
See \url{ada/open_team_shaping.md}, lines 73--98.

The original note gives two accounting repairs.
Full-time accounting retains all potential transitions for each permanent identity, including absences, while restricting action log-probabilities to actual decisions.
Alternatively, an interface that splits active lives can reset potentials to zero at reentry and exit, retaining exit transitions.
The latter deliberately modifies softmax normalization at those boundaries and preserves only that interface's base objective; it does not resolve a separate mismatch between per-life and team returns.
Both require defined singleton/empty-coalition behavior and reuse of realized boundary potentials.
\ledger{5, 6}
See \url{ada/open_team_shaping.md}, lines 100--105.

\paragraph{The original estimator objection.}
\begin{objection}
Connected comparisons do not suffice for the stated finite-error guarantee of P's unregularized Bradley--Terry MLE.
For two agents with centered true scores \((0,0)\), two independent comparisons produce the same winner with probability \(1/2\).
The undirected comparison graph is connected, but on this event the likelihood has no finite maximizer.
This exceeds the proposition's stated failure probability \(2^{-2}=1/4\).
\ledger{3}
\end{objection}

The method specifies neither a score bound nor a regularizer, although the proof invokes regularized estimation.
The main proposition and appendix also disagree about normalized versus unnormalized error, and the final assembly loses dimension dependence.
A repaired statement needs an identified estimator, translation convention, sampling assumptions, score-range control, and graph dependence.
Model-correct comparison sampling does not establish robustness to arbitrary hallucinations or systematic observational bias.
The Shapley interpretation additionally assumes that the latent comparator scores already equal Shapley values.
Repairing these statements would still not prove a policy-return guarantee.
\ledger{3, 6}
See \url{ada/open_team_shaping.md}, lines 108--114.

\paragraph{First-round objections and their disposition.}
The investigation accepted that objective neutrality was established prior work.
Grzes's terminal-boundary analysis also limits any novelty claim for the mask identity.
The possible contribution became an audit of open-team interfaces rather than a new telescoping theorem.
\ledger{4, 5, 6}

Implementation attribution remained unresolved.
P does not specify whether rewards, continuation returns, actor losses, or episode boundaries are masked.
An actor-loss mask alone does not imply the counterexample: continuation returns might propagate correctly through absences.
No MARS-RA implementation was obtained or executed.
\ledger{2, 6}

The first note therefore supplied an exact counterexample and an estimator objection, but no verified implementation defect or demonstrated training consequence.
The verifier returned \emph{ITERATE}.
Entry 8 requested a bounded public-code audit, an exact and actor-critic attribution experiment, and a finite-budget estimator analysis, explicitly allowing clean or null outcomes.
The following second-round results answer that request.
\ledger{6, 8, 9, 16}

\paragraph{Round-two addition: clean return interfaces.}
The audit inspected pinned public snapshots, with provenance and file manifests under
\url{ada/audit/}.
No inspected repository was edited or trained in this audit.
\ledger{10, 15}
The audit record is
\url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 15--41.

In XuanCe,
\url{xuance/common/memory_tools_marl.py}, lines 210--236,
computes returns from stored rewards and terminal flags without multiplying by \texttt{agent\_mask}.
MAPPO uses that mask in actor and critic losses instead.
In the standard MAPPO implementation,
\url{onpolicy/runner/shared/smac_runner.py}, lines 133--152,
distinguishes environment-terminal return masks from individual-agent \texttt{active\_masks}.
Its return buffer uses the former.
These are clean against the specific hypothesis that an activity mask automatically discards shaping rewards.
They do not establish how P's custom environment supplies rewards or terminal flags.
\ledger{10, 15}

Source-extracted return methods from both implementations were executed on the original absence/reentry trace.
Both return initial shaping return \(-0.5\) when supplied full shaping, regardless of the activity mask.
They return \(-0.905\) only when the caller first multiplies shaping rewards by the activity mask.
Results are in
\url{ada/probe_clean_interfaces_01.log}, lines 1--3.
These are positive trace checks, not a complete audit of every truncation option or environment.
\ledger{15}

The inspected SAMA public snapshot contains prompts and documentation but no Python training implementation.
It therefore does not support a reward/GAE audit.
This does not establish that P lacked another implementation.
Targeted searches did not locate MARS-RA or the embodied MARS-Bench training code.
\ledger{10, 15}
See \url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 35--41 and 223--227.

\paragraph{Round-two addition: a reproduced adjacent-code defect.}
The V-GEPF public snapshot is different.
Its shaping kernel flattens worker trajectories, shifts the potential array globally, and only then restores worker/time dimensions.
It hardcodes discount \(0.995\) and does not consult \texttt{DONE}, while the return estimator cuts GAE continuation at \texttt{DONE}.
The environment also uses scoring events as segment boundaries.
The rollout audit establishes that the affected last shaping reward is consumed rather than discarded as a bootstrap element.
\ledger{11, 12}

The source locations, relative to the pinned repository, are
\url{light_malib/algorithm/mappo/trainer.py}, lines 278--287;
\url{light_malib/algorithm/return_compute.py}, lines 85--100;
and \url{light_malib/rollout/rollout_func.py}, lines 127--145.
The explanation and provenance are in
\url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 43--77.

With matching discount, row length \(T\), and no internal boundary, the flattened shift produces, for every worker except the last,
\[
\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^t F_{b,t}
=-\Phi_{b,0}+\gamma^T\Phi_{b+1,0}.
\]
The next worker's first potential is not the current worker's terminal potential.
The last worker instead receives a zero destination.
Thus the kernel introduces dependence on an unrelated trajectory.
\ledger{11, 12}

The regression probe executes unedited extracted shaping and raw-GAE statements on supplied post-normalization potentials.
It uses \emph{synthetic \(T=3\) trajectories}, not model inference, Ray, or football.
Changing only worker 2's initial potential from \(0.8\) to \(0.1\) changes worker 1's last shaping reward and root raw GAE:
\[
\begin{array}{lrr}
 & 0.8 & 0.1\\
\hline
\text{Last shaping reward} & 0.496 & -0.2005\\
\text{Root raw GAE} & 0.63528192 & 0.01296086
\end{array}
\]
For the same synthetic \(T=3\) probe, permuting trajectories and applying the actual batch-wide advantage-normalization formula changes a simple root Bernoulli surrogate ascent gradient from \(0.54852288\) to \(0.67915906\).
This is the unclipped PPO surrogate derivative at the old policy on synthetic data, not a measured football training gradient.
The test establishes dependence of consumed rewards and advantages on batch order.
Its practical interpretation is downgraded by the third-round scale checks below.
\ledger{12, 17, 19}
See \url{ada/probe_vgepf_03.log}, lines 1--11.

There is also a configuration-dependent discount mismatch.
The academy configurations for
\url{3_vs_1_with_keeper},
\url{pass_and_shoot_with_keeper}, and
\url{run_pass_and_shoot_with_keeper}
use learner discount \(0.99\), whereas the kernel uses \(0.995\).
The corrected record states that
\url{academy/corner/mappo.yaml}
uses \(0.995\), and
\url{full_game/11_vs_11_easy/mappo_real.yaml}
uses \(0.999\).
The earlier inspected principal full-game configuration uses \(0.995\).
For a unit potential over 100 transitions, the kernel evaluated at learner discount \(0.99\) gives discounted shaping return \(-0.68486482\) instead of \(-1\).
The mismatch is real in the tested arithmetic but not universal across configurations.
\ledger{11, 12, 17, 19}
See \url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, line 73, and
\url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, line 39.

Shifting within each worker's time axis, matching discounts, and zeroing destinations at \emph{true} terminal boundaries removes the corresponding demonstrated defects.
A truncation that should bootstrap requires the actual next potential instead of forced zero.
The third-round correction is essential: a score-induced GAE cut need not terminate the game, so zeroing every scoring destination is not automatically a repair.
The intended full-game or segment objective must first be specified.
No benchmark performance effect has been measured, and the inspected revision has not been shown to match P's adapted V-GEPF baseline.
Nothing here establishes a MARS-RA implementation defect.
\ledger{12, 15, 23}

\paragraph{Round-two addition: controlled attribution experiment.}
The simulator contains a focal agent and a passive teammate.
The focal agent makes a single nontrivial decision, Stay or Recharge, at the root.
Success at an eight-transition horizon has probability \(0.50\) or \(0.55\), respectively; the only environmental reward is terminal \(+1\) or \(-1\).
Recharge independently causes one-step absences at times \(2,4,6\), each with probability \(q\), followed by reentry.
With \(\gamma=0.99\), its true return advantage is
\[
\Delta J=0.1\gamma^7=0.093206534790699.
\]
The schedule is represented in the subsequent tabular state.
This is an exactly solvable diagnostic, not a realistic battery model or a MARS-Bench replication.
\ledger{12, 14}
See \url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 79--105.

The declared exact comparator assigns focal-agent potential \(0.75\) after Recharge and \(0.25\) after Stay, with common root potential \(0.5\).
Reversed comparisons interchange the two values.
A frozen random comparator uses the empirical share of two fair judgments per state; a uniform comparator assigns \(0.5\).
Inactive focal-agent potential is zero.
``Exact'' refers only to this declared target, not to verified Shapley values or an actual LMM.
All accounting modes retain the same environmental rewards.

Exact enumeration crosses these comparators with full-time, active-masked, and entry-reset accounting, four turnover settings, and six shaping weights: 288 cases.
For constant recharge potential \(p\), active masking changes the policy gap to
\[
\Delta\widehat J
=0.1\gamma^7-\rho q p
  \left(\gamma^3+\gamma^5+\gamma^7\right).
\]
This follows directly by charging each possible reentry.
A larger intended contribution can therefore worsen the incorrect objective by increasing entry penalties.
Full-time and entry-reset accounting preserve the base objective in every enumerated case.
\ledger{12, 14}

The learning experiment uses a tabular root actor, a tabular critic, GAE with \(\lambda=0.95\), and batch updates.
It retains all global-time critic transitions.
There are 144 configurations, each with 32 seeds and 12,800 episodes, crossing comparator, accounting, turnover, shaping weight, and critic initialization.
Random-number streams are shared across variants.
It is not MAPPO.
\ledger{14}
The configuration and results are described in
\url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 103--123, with outputs under
\url{ada/attribution_01/}.

At \(q=0.75\), \(\rho=1\), exact comparisons, and ordinary zero critic initialization, the selected results are
\[
\begin{array}{lrr}
\text{Accounting} & \text{Shaped gap} &
  \Pr(\text{Recharge})\\
\hline
\text{Full time} & 0.09321 & 0.93202\pm0.00433\\
\text{Active masked} & -1.51181 & 0.00340\pm0.00007\\
\text{Entry reset} & 0.09321 & 0.93156\pm0.00428
\end{array}
\]
Uncertainty is standard error across seeds.
The logs preserve unrounded records:
\url{ada/check_report_numbers_01.log}, lines 1--3.
These outcomes demonstrate a learning consequence of the constructed objective distortion, not its prevalence in deployed systems.
\ledger{14}

Correct accounting also permits a small finite-training benefit.
With zero turnover, \(\rho=1\), and zero critics, the mean Recharge probability across training updates is \(0.8213\) for exact comparisons, \(0.7984\) for reversed comparisons, and \(0.8079\) without shaping.
The paired exact-minus-unshaped difference is \(0.01348\pm0.00208\) standard error.
Thus the experiment does not support the claim that every ranking benefit is an accounting artifact.
\ledger{14, 15}
See \url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 119--123.

\paragraph{The argument behind the critic controls.}
Let \(c_t\) be the continuation flag used by the return estimator, and write
\[
\begin{aligned}
F_t^{\mathrm{used}}
  &=\gamma c_t\Phi_{t+1}-\Phi_t+e_t,\\
V_t^{\mathrm{sh}}
  &=V_t-\rho\Phi_t+h_t.
\end{aligned}
\]
Substitution into the two TD errors gives the exact identity
\[
\delta_t^{\mathrm{sh}}-\delta_t
=\rho e_t+\gamma c_t h_{t+1}-h_t.
\]
For a finite rollout, define
\[
w_{t,\ell}
=(\gamma\lambda)^\ell
 \prod_{u=t}^{t+\ell-1}c_u.
\]
With compatible bootstrap values, the GAE difference is
\[
\begin{aligned}
A_t^{\mathrm{sh}}-A_t
={}&\sum_{\ell=0}^{H-t-1}w_{t,\ell}
\Bigl(
 \rho e_{t+\ell}\\
&\hspace{24mm}
 +\gamma c_{t+\ell}h_{t+\ell+1}
 -h_{t+\ell}
\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\]
This separates an interface residual \(e_t\) from critic mismatch \(h_t\).
There is no automatic additional term translating comparison error into policy-return error.
\ledger{14, 15}
The derivation is in
\url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 125--160.

With coherent accounting and a matched shifted critic, both residuals vanish and TD errors agree pathwise.
In the tabular experiment, the representation
\[
V^{\mathrm{sh}}=W-\rho\Phi,
\qquad W_{\mathrm{initial}}=0,
\]
makes every correct-accounting learning curve coincide with its unshaped counterpart to maximum absolute error \(1.21\times10^{-14}\).
For ordinary zero critics, a terminal-zero trajectory without interior cuts has initial shaping contribution to root GAE
\[
\rho\left[
-\Phi_0+\gamma(1-\lambda)
\sum_{t=1}^{H-1}(\gamma\lambda)^{t-1}\Phi_t
\right].
\]
This displays a bootstrap mechanism; the formula alone does not establish that its effect is transient.
At \(\lambda=1\), only the common root baseline remains.
The separate single-decision control run makes all full-accounting comparator curves coincide to \(2.61\times10^{-14}\).
\ledger{14, 28, 30}

These are attribution controls within an established equivalence between shaping and value initialization.
They are not a new general convergence theorem.
A neural critic need not realize the exact potential shift, particularly if the predictor changes or its realized output is unavailable to the critic.
The single-decision \(\lambda=1\) result does not transfer automatically to general MARL.
The third-round neural experiment explicitly checks this distinction.
\ledger{14, 15, 25}

\paragraph{Round-two addition: what two-agent aggregation estimates.}
For two active agents, let \(W\) be the total wins plus half-ties among \(K\) judgments.
Writing \(p=\sigma(c_1-c_2)\), the likelihood is proportional to
\[
L(p)=p^W(1-p)^{K-W}.
\]
For \(0<W<K\), maximizing it gives
\[
\operatorname{softmax}(\widehat{\boldsymbol c})
=\left(\frac WK,1-\frac WK\right).
\]
At \(W=0\) or \(W=K\), the finite logit MLE does not exist, but the normalized potential has the corresponding finite limit.
Thus P's default two-agent budget, one query for each ordering, yields only
\[
\{0,1/2,1\}
\quad\text{or, with half-ties,}\quad
\{0,1/4,1/2,3/4,1\}
\]
for the first potential.
There is no transitivity information to aggregate in this case.
A direct empirical-win-share control reproduces the stated potential, apart from numerical fitting or regularization conventions.
\ledger{13, 15}
See \url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 162--173.

Under independent fair binary comparisons, exhaustive enumeration at two judgments per unordered pair gives
\[
\begin{array}{crr}
\text{Active agents} & K & \Pr(\text{no finite MLE})\\
\hline
2 & 2 & 1/2\\
3 & 6 & 9/32\\
4 & 12 & 61/512
\end{array}
\]
The test uses strong connectivity of the directed observed-win graph, not connectivity of the undirected comparison design.
If P intended the former condition, it must be stated and its probability addressed.
These counts also assume that both ordered judgments are accumulated rather than overwritten.
They do not assume actual LMM judgments are independent or fair.
\ledger{13}
See \url{ada/bt_budget_01.log}, lines 13--18, and
\url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 175--183.

For independent binary judgments with preference probability \(p^\star\), the extended potential estimate satisfies
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[(\widehat p-p^\star)^2]
  &=\frac{p^\star(1-p^\star)}K,\\
\Pr(\text{no finite MLE})
  &=(p^\star)^K+(1-p^\star)^K.
\end{aligned}
\]
At \(K=2\), nonexistence probabilities are \(0.5\), \(0.625\), and \(0.82\) for \(p^\star=0.5,0.75,0.9\).
More decisive comparisons can increase logit nonexistence while improving normalized-probability estimation.
The consumed potential is therefore a more useful repair target than logit error alone.
\ledger{13, 15}

The notes also record ordinary smoothing:
\[
\widehat p_\alpha=\frac{W+\alpha}{K+2\alpha},
\qquad \alpha>0,
\]
with exact binary-model risk
\[
\mathbb E[(\widehat p_\alpha-p^\star)^2]
=
\frac{Kp^\star(1-p^\star)
+\alpha^2(1-2p^\star)^2}
{(K+2\alpha)^2}.
\]
If at most \(\epsilon K\) clean independent judgments are arbitrarily replaced, Hoeffding's inequality and the replacement bound give, with probability at least \(1-\delta\),
\[
\begin{aligned}
|\widehat p_\alpha-p^\star|
\leq{}&
\frac{K}{K+2\alpha}
\left(
\epsilon+\sqrt{\frac{\log(2/\delta)}{2K}}
\right)\\
&+\frac{\alpha}{K+2\alpha}.
\end{aligned}
\]
This is a standard estimation repair, not a new ranking method.
Half-tie observations require interpreting their mean as a tie-adjusted preference probability.
Perfectly correlated repeated judgments do not obtain the independent-sample variance reduction.
None of these statements proves a learned-policy return bound or demonstrates a numerical failure in P's implementation.
\ledger{13, 15}
See \url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 185--211.

\paragraph{External sources used in the first two rounds.}
The prior-work checks constrain the interpretation.
\ledger{4, 5, 6, 15}
\begin{itemize}
\item Devlin and Kudenko, \emph{Dynamic Potential-Based Reward Shaping}, establishes dynamic-potential telescoping and objective/Nash invariance:
\url{https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/75121/2/p433_devlin.pdf}.
\item Gupta et al., \emph{Behavior Alignment via Reward Function Optimization}, Property 1, establishes unchanged expected policy gradients and potentially increased variance under potential-based shaping:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19007}.
\item Grzes, \emph{Reward Shaping in Episodic Reinforcement Learning}, analyzes terminal residuals and multi-agent consequences:
\url{https://www.ifaamas.org/Proceedings/aamas2017/pdfs/p565.pdf}.
\item Wiewiora, \emph{Potential-Based Shaping and Q-Value Initialization are Equivalent}, establishes tabular value-update equivalence:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1106.5267}.
\item M\"uller and Kudenko discuss initial values, potential offsets, and sample efficiency:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01307}.
\item V-GEPF's own appendix assumes terminal-zero potentials and discusses shifted value initialization:
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.13430}.
Its inspected code mismatch is a theorem-to-implementation issue.
\item Gao and Toni treat hierarchical and semi-Markov shaping:
\url{https://ijcai.org/proceedings/2015/493}.
A variable-duration discount correction alone is not a new direction.
\item Turner and Firth's BradleyTerry2 work supports bias-reduced estimation:
\url{https://www.jstatsoft.org/article/view/v048i09}.
Rinaldo, Petrovi\'c, and Fienberg describe the observed-win connectivity criterion:
\url{https://www.stat.cmu.edu/~arinaldo/papers/Rinaldo_Petrovic_Fienberg.pdf}.
\end{itemize}

Retrieval details are in
\url{ada/open_team_shaping.md}, lines 128--152, and
\url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 213--227.
The shared pool supplied LaTeX for Gupta et al., V-GEPF, and M\"uller--Kudenko.
Devlin--Kudenko, Grzes, and Wiewiora were checked through pooled PDF text after LaTeX source was unavailable.
Pool lookups for Gao--Toni, Turner--Firth, and Rinaldo et al.\ failed during metadata resolution.
Their use was limited to primary-host excerpts or publisher pages; no full-source review or alternate PDF extraction is claimed.

The public-code snapshots were:
\begin{itemize}
\item XuanCe:
\url{https://github.com/agi-brain/xuance/tree/63eca3216bd87055de55ff89e94fe64f31a80e3d}.
\item Standard MAPPO:
\url{https://github.com/marlbenchmark/on-policy/tree/de66d7a4b23fac2513f56f96f73b3f5cb96695ac}.
\item SAMA:
\url{https://github.com/ewanlee/SAMA/tree/e1e21cd9358b40163e09a496584461a8331aee27}.
\item V-GEPF:
\url{https://github.com/Marroh/V-GEPF-official-code/tree/406a2c06fb836c8f1350a01e84d37b08900c699f}.
\end{itemize}
The searches, including inspection of
\url{https://aclanthology.org/2026.acl-long.1349/},
did not locate MARS-RA training code.
This is a bounded search outcome, not evidence of nonavailability.
No message or code request was sent to the authors.
\ledger{6, 15}

\paragraph{What the second round resolved and left open.}
The second round supplied a public learned-shaping baseline with a reproduced batch-boundary defect that changes consumed advantages.
It also supplied counterevidence to indiscriminate attribution: the tested XuanCe and standard MAPPO return paths preserve full shaping through activity masks.
The controlled study established both masking-induced objective reversal and a finite-training benefit that survives correct accounting.
Matched-critic controls separated the latter from an objective improvement.
\ledger{10, 12, 14, 15}

However, there was no measured football benchmark effect, no proof that the inspected revision matched published experiments or P's adaptation, and no access to P's wrapper or estimator.
The algebra and estimator repairs did not warrant a standalone theory claim.
The supported candidate was an attribution and reproducibility audit, not a new consistency--robustness frontier, a MARS-RA replication failure, or proof that semantic credit was unnecessary.
\ledger{8, 15, 16}
See \url{ada/round2_attribution.md}, lines 229--235.

The previous note proposed a short paired football rerun and a P-specific exit/reentry trace.
Verifier entry 17 judged the football rerun poor value before a scale check, and found the one-decision toy inadequate to address large gains in sparse cooperative learning.
That objection was accepted.
The third round replaced the immediate football priority with scale measurements, a neural exploration diagnostic, and a reusable conformance audit.
The original P-specific trace remains useful if its code becomes available.
\ledger{17, 18}

\paragraph{Round-three addition: scale of the V-GEPF effect.}
The longer-rollout probe executes the extracted shaping and GAE kernels with 32 worker rows, \(\gamma=0.995\), \(\lambda=0.95\), and lengths \(T\in\{3,400,3001\}\).
It supplies smooth sinusoidal potentials with either common initial value \(0.5\) or initial values in \([0.1,0.9]\), and synthetic segment-end rewards of \(\pm1\).
These stand in for post-normalization outputs; they are neither measured CLIP outputs nor a bound on deployed potentials.
The source standardizes and scales its potentials.
The tests include no interior cuts, two jittered interior cuts, and a stress schedule with a cut every 100 steps.
None is claimed to reproduce football scoring statistics.
\ledger{19, 23, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 15--39, and
\url{ada/scale_vgepf_results.json}.

A row-end reward perturbation reaches the root raw GAE with multiplier
\[
(\gamma\lambda)^{T-1}.
\]
It is approximately
\[
\begin{aligned}
T=400 &: \quad 1.75\times10^{-10},\\
T=3001 &: \quad 4.37\times10^{-74}.
\end{aligned}
\]
This sharply limits the practical reading of the earlier synthetic \(T=3\) root-gradient example.
Nearby advantages still change, and batch-wide normalization can spread changes in mean or scale.
Such spreading alone does not establish an expected policy-gradient effect; action-score correlations also matter.
\ledger{17, 19, 26}

With varying initial potentials and no interior cuts, the reported changes are
\[
\begin{array}{lrr}
 & T=400 & T=3001\\
\hline
\text{Mean raw correction magnitude}
 &0.01901&0.002534\\
\Pr(|\text{raw change}|>0.01)
 &0.1563&0.0208\\
\text{Mean normalized permutation change}
 &0.07725&0.02603
\end{array}
\]
The permutation calculation undoes the row permutation before comparing advantages.
With common initial potentials, the corresponding normalized permutation changes are \(0.008705\) and \(0.002936\), mainly because a different final row receives a zero successor.
These are synthetic scale-dependent quantities, not benchmark effects.
\ledger{19, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 29--31.

With two jittered interior cuts, mean absolute raw advantage differences relative to declaring the cut destinations terminal are \(0.04460\) and \(0.006108\) at the two longer lengths.
With cuts every 100 steps, they are \(0.06862\) and \(0.09073\).
The local discrepancy at a cut contains
\[
\gamma\Phi_{t+1}.
\]
Its interpretation depends on the intended return.
\ledger{23, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 33--37.

In particular,
\url{light_malib/rollout/rollout_func.py}, lines 368--389,
resets only when \texttt{env.is\_terminated()} is true.
A score-induced \texttt{DONE} can therefore cut GAE without terminating the game.
The discrepancy is then with the return estimator's continuation convention, not automatically with full-game potential telescoping.
Zeroing the potential at every score can itself change the full-game objective.
This resolves the earlier ambiguity by separating true-terminal cross-worker leakage from interior return segmentation.
\ledger{23}

If successor initial potentials and row durations are policy independent, much of the row-end return residual is also policy independent and need not change policy ordering.
Scoring times and destination states may instead be policy dependent, but their actual residual distribution was not measured.
The source-level defect stands; its practical significance remains unresolved and is downgraded.
No football rerun was undertaken.
\ledger{19, 23, 26}

\paragraph{Round-three addition: a neural exploration diagnostic.}
The next experiment implements a MAPPO-style learner with shared decentralized neural actors, a centralized per-agent critic, clipped PPO updates, and GAE.
It does not import the standard MAPPO repository, reproduce P's architecture, use an LMM, or implement an embodied simulator.
\ledger{22, 24, 25}
The task and implementation are described in
\url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 41--90, and
\url{ada/neural_attribution.py}.

Each of two agents receives an episode-specific binary cue identifying its correct action.
A correct action increments its progress counter; an incorrect action resets the counter.
Completion is retained once a target run length is reached.
The team receives \(+1\) only if both agents finish by horizon 24, and \(-1\) otherwise, with no intermediate environmental reward.
Thus random policies must discover consecutive correct actions for both agents.
Cooperation is a shared AND objective, not physical interaction or strategic role division.
The optimal cue-following local rule is simple once learning obtains a useful signal.
\ledger{25, 26}

Actors observe their own progress, cue, activity, identity, and time, but not the teammate's progress or cue.
The full centralized critic can observe both agents' state.
An active unfinished agent becomes absent for two decisions with probability \(0.1\) per step and then reenters; completed agents do not leave.
All global-time rewards and critic transitions are retained.
Only inactive and completed actors are excluded from action losses.
This deliberately implements the clean full-time contract.
\ledger{22, 25}

The main task requires six consecutive correct actions.
After interim results showed that its baseline was often learnable, a sensitivity task changed only the required run length to eight, without retuning hyperparameters.
Both tasks and all outcomes were retained.
Exact finite-state checks give initial random-policy success probabilities
\[
\begin{aligned}
L=6 &: \quad 0.01529645,\\
L=8 &: \quad 0.000766384.
\end{aligned}
\]
Always following the cue succeeds with probability one in either task.
Always choosing the same binary action succeeds on the one-quarter of episodes where both cues match it, explaining a simple suboptimal plateau.
\ledger{22, 25, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 47--53, and
\url{ada/check_neural_task_01.log}.

\paragraph{The neural prediction interface and controls.}
For two active agents with progress counters \(x_t^1,x_t^2\), the declared comparison probability is
\[
p_t^\star=\sigma\!\left(0.8(x_t^1-x_t^2)\right),
\qquad
\Phi_t=(p_t,1-p_t).
\]
``Exact'' means \(p_t=p_t^\star\) for this declared progress comparator, not a true Shapley value or uniquely correct causal contribution.
The two-query condition uses the empirical mean of two independent Bernoulli draws with parameter \(p_t^\star\), matching P's two-agent comparison count and the extended BT softmax estimate without ties.
Reversed comparisons use \(1-p_t^\star\), random comparisons use two fair draws, and uniform potentials use \(1/2\).
A 16-query condition reduces sampling variance around the same probability.
\ledger{22, 25}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 55--74.

Absent agents have potential zero; a sole active agent has potential one; both absent means both potentials are zero.
All horizon potentials are zero.
Each realized potential is sampled once and reused in both adjacent differences.
Consequently, stochastic comparison noise and turnover do not break the pathwise return identity.

Actors and critics are separate two-hidden-layer tanh networks of width 32 with zero-initialized output layers.
Training uses 160 updates, 64 complete episodes per update, and three full-batch PPO/critic epochs.
The actor and critic learning rates are \(3\times10^{-4}\) and \(10^{-3}\), PPO clipping is \(0.2\), entropy coefficient is \(0.01\), gradient clipping is \(0.5\), and \(\gamma=0.99\).
Runs use CPU float64, eight seeds per condition, and 2,048 fresh stochastic-policy evaluation episodes.
Action/environment streams are paired by seed and comparison draws use a separate stream; different policies need not visit identical states.
\ledger{25}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 76--90.

The matrix includes \(\lambda\in\{0.95,1\}\), \(\rho\in\{0,1,5\}\), full and blinded critics, potential-input controls, shifted-head controls, and additional query-budget and zero-turnover cases.
It is not a complete Cartesian product.
A blinded critic sees time, its own activity, and identity, but cannot observe progress, cues, or teammate activity.
The shifted-head control uses
\[
V_\theta^{\mathrm{sh}}(s,i)
=W_\theta(s,i)-\rho\Phi_t^i,
\]
with the same stored potential realization as the shaping reward and no gradient through the predictor.
Matched inputs, initialization, and optimization allow a pathwise comparison with the unshaped learner.
Every declared configuration is recorded in the run directories' \url{plan.json} files.
The distinction between own activity and joint activity is required for interpreting the later random-comparator controls.
\ledger{22, 25, 35}

\paragraph{What the neural GAE check establishes.}
A separately trained unshaped shadow critic is evaluated on the same collected trajectories.
Define
\[
h_t=V_t^{\mathrm{sh}}-V_t^{\mathrm{ref}}+\rho\Phi_t,
\qquad h_H=0.
\]
With exact accounting and no interior continuation cuts, summing the TD-error difference gives
\[
A_t^{\mathrm{sh}}-A_t^{\mathrm{ref}}
=-h_t+\gamma(1-\lambda)
\sum_{j=t+1}^{H-1}
(\gamma\lambda)^{j-t-1}h_j.
\]
This is the earlier GAE identity specialized to complete episodes.
The experiment checks it and the discounted return-offset identity on every collected episode, and logs RMS critic and advantage discrepancies.
\ledger{24, 25}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 92--113.

At \(\lambda=1\), the difference is the pre-action baseline \(-h_t\).
Under the usual causal sampling assumptions its expected policy-score contribution is zero, but it can alter finite-sample variance, advantage normalization, and subsequent clipped PPO updates.
Therefore ordinary neural learning curves need not coincide at \(\lambda=1\).
The shifted-head control is stronger: it cancels the TD and GAE differences pathwise at every tested \(\lambda\).
\ledger{25}

An incapable critic can produce persistent optimization difficulties within its function class.
It cannot make a correctly telescoping reward transformation change the trajectory-return objective.
Likewise, RMS discrepancy alone does not determine whether learning improves; its direction and correlation with useful action scores matter.
These qualifications prevent an algebraic identity from becoming an unsupported learning guarantee.
The original interpretation of a failed finite run as merely transient was untested; the fourth round below investigates that issue.
\ledger{25, 26, 28, 30}

\paragraph{Neural results under correct accounting.}
The selected final evaluation results below use the full critic and, for shaped runs, \(\rho=1\).
Uncertainty is standard error across eight seeds.
Let \(S_0\) denote unshaped success and \(S_{\mathrm{exact}}\) exact-comparator success:
\[
\begin{array}{ccrr}
L&\lambda&S_0&S_{\mathrm{exact}}\\
\hline
6&0.95&0.7651\pm0.1205&0.9970\pm0.0021\\
6&1&0.4343\pm0.1230&0.5317\pm0.1362\\
8&0.95&0.2429\pm0.1122&0.7124\pm0.1377\\
8&1&0.06262\pm0.03811&0.00122\pm0.00039
\end{array}
\]
The corresponding sampled and reversed-comparator results are
\[
\begin{array}{ccrr}
L&\lambda&S_{\mathrm{two\ queries}}&S_{\mathrm{reversed}}\\
\hline
6&0.95&0.8340\pm0.1099&0.2266\pm0.0324\\
6&1&0.4371\pm0.1215&0.4362\pm0.1219\\
8&0.95&0.4136\pm0.1278&0.0527\pm0.0356\\
8&1&0.02716\pm0.02633&0.05658\pm0.03512
\end{array}
\]
\ledger{24, 25}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 153--168, and
\url{ada/neural_summary_final.json}.

At length eight and \(\lambda=0.95\), the paired final exact-minus-unshaped difference is \(0.4695\pm0.1573\).
During updates 71--80, mean training success is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Exact comparisons}:&\quad 0.17617\pm0.02111,\\
\text{Two-query comparisons}:&\quad 0.03008\pm0.00992,\\
\text{No shaping}:&\quad 0.000391\pm0.000256.
\end{aligned}
\]
The paired exact-minus-unshaped difference at this intermediate budget is \(0.17578\pm0.02117\).
Thus more-than-order-of-magnitude gains at an intermediate training budget are possible under correct accounting.
The relative size partly reflects a near-zero baseline, and the final relative improvement is much smaller.
This is neither a reproduction nor an explanation of P's particular gains.
\ledger{25, 26}

For length six, some baseline seeds solve the task.
The final paired difference is \(0.2319\pm0.1195\), so an unqualified statistical-superiority claim from that estimate would be too strong.
Mean training success over the first 80 updates improves by \(0.04104\pm0.01178\) paired standard error.
All seeds are retained.
\ledger{24, 25}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 164--166.

At P's two-query budget, the final paired gains over the unshaped learner were not statistically distinguishable in this experiment: approximately \(0.069\pm0.044\) for length six and \(0.171\pm0.187\) for length eight.
Verifier entry 28 explicitly required this qualification.
\ledger{28}

At \(\lambda=1\), exact shaping harms some cases rather than merely losing its benefit.
For length six, its paired training-success difference during updates 71--80 is \(-0.084\pm0.029\).
For length eight, final success is \(0.00122\) shaped versus \(0.06262\) unshaped.
Ordinary neural curves therefore do not coincide, and raw expected-gradient invariance does not imply finite-training invariance.
Exact coincidences occur in the matched shifted-head controls: all 48 matched pairs have identical stored training-success curves and evaluation success, including exact and noisy comparisons where tested, both GAE settings, and both difficulty levels.
This validates an attribution control founded on established equivalence, not a new invariance theorem.
\ledger{25, 28, 30}

With a blinded critic on length six at \(\lambda=0.95\), mean success is \(0.6202\) without shaping and \(0.9910\) with exact comparisons, while reversed comparisons give zero success in all eight runs.
All satisfy the return-offset check.
Giving the realized potential to the critic reduces measured mismatch but does not uniformly improve learning: exact-comparator success is \(0.8948\) with potential input, compared with \(0.9970\) for the ordinary full critic and \(0.7841\) for the matched unshaped input-critic baseline.
These small-seed, overlapping estimates are descriptive, not an architecture ranking.
\ledger{25, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 170--172.

The harder task also shows why RMS mismatch is insufficient.
At \(\lambda=0.95\), two-query and random comparators have similar final-window RMS mismatch, approximately \(0.294\) and \(0.300\), but final success is approximately \(0.414\) and \(0.00085\).
The GAE identity locates the influence; it does not determine whether that influence is useful.
\ledger{25}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, line 172.

All 60 configurations completed with eight seeds each: 480 runs and 4,915,200 training episodes.
These counts exclude the pilot and evaluation episodes.
The maximum GAE-identity error was \(8.66\times10^{-15}\), and the maximum discounted telescoping error was \(1.07\times10^{-14}\).
Completeness, finiteness, and shifted-pair assertions passed.
\ledger{25}
See \url{ada/check_round3_results_01.log}, lines 1--14.
Run outputs are under
\url{ada/neural_main_01/},
\url{ada/neural_controls_01/}, and
\url{ada/neural_hard_01/};
versions and script hashes are in
\url{ada/round3_provenance.json}.

\paragraph{Round-three addition: reusable conformance tests.}
The expanded audit is a convenience sample, not a prevalence estimate.
It begins with implementations named by P or its baselines and adds accessible Python implementations found by targeted searches for learned potential shaping.
Implementations that merely replace rewards without an additive PBRS claim are outside this preservation contract.
The inclusion rule and searches are recorded in
\url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 115--141.
\ledger{21, 26}

The suite
\url{ada/conformance_suite.py}
extracts relevant methods from syntax trees, removes annotations, and stubs external inference.
It tests worker-row permutation, true terminal handling, artificial interior cuts, actual-next-potential bootstrap handling, discount agreement, full-time absence/reentry, and a realized-potential-reuse fixture.
Passing one fixture is not repository certification.
Artificial respawn or interior-terminal fixtures for text or time-series kernels do not imply that those applications contain agent turnover.
\ledger{21, 26}

The XuanCe and standard MAPPO return checks remain clean.
V-GEPF has mixed results: row permutation and discount agreement fail, and the kernel lacks the actual next potential at row truncations; its single-worker terminal-zero and supplied full-time reentry traces pass.
The single-worker pass occurs because the last flattened successor is zero.
Interior \texttt{DONE} results require the full-game versus segment distinction already stated.
\ledger{19, 21, 23, 26}

\paragraph{PSPO: an additional consumed terminal residual.}
The inspected snapshot is
\url{https://github.com/wandugu/paper_pspo/tree/47cc5818fa161aea36105f67e55ae668430d1f27}.
Its README claims a terminal-zero absorbing state.
In
\url{src/llamafactory/train/pspo/potential.py},
the feature builder creates response-length-plus-one state features and marks them valid.
The shaping method, lines 314--317, appends zero to shifted potentials but slices away that appended zero before computing differences.
The final emitted reward therefore uses a learned completed-response potential instead of zero.
\ledger{21}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 129--133.

Both the actual feature builder and actual shaping method were executed on synthetic hidden states sharing the same prompt.
At \(\gamma=1\), the required common shaping offset is \(-0.1\), while the observed sums are
\[
\begin{array}{crr}
\text{Response length}&\text{Shaping sum}&\text{Terminal residual}\\
\hline
1&0.05&0.15\\
3&0.15&0.25
\end{array}
\]
These are reachable arithmetic values under the inference stubs, not measurements from trained PSPO weights.
The source path consumes the shaped token rewards:
\url{ppo/trainer.py}, lines 454--457, returns them, and lines 278--285 pass them to PPO.
Row-permutation and supplied-discount tests pass.
Neither published-model performance nor publication status was established by this audit.
\ledger{21, 26}
See \url{ada/conformance_suite_02.log}, lines 8--9.

\paragraph{Clean and qualified additional cases.}
Reward-Shaping-LLM was inspected at
\url{https://github.com/baharehgl/Reward-Shaping-LLM/tree/2e009980398b797ae7acefc7497ab5e368673783}.
Its kernel evaluates consecutive state potentials without a terminal argument.
It passes row-permutation, supplied-discount, and actual-next-potential truncation checks but retains a finite terminal residual.
However, the inspected time-series cursor advances independently of the chosen anomaly label.
For a fixed series and fixed cached predictor, that endpoint residual is action independent and does not itself change policy ordering.
A failed strict terminal-zero check is therefore insufficient evidence of behavioral harm.
Stochastic provider behavior and cache eviction were not tested.
\ledger{21, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, line 135.

PRM4RL was inspected at
\url{https://github.com/deng-ai-lab/PRM4RL/tree/f9727ed0d62aa7106b1dd8d76a52b5a6d6775079}.
Its progress differences replace environmental reward, are clipped, and receive a success bonus.
It is excluded from the additive PBRS conformance denominator.
Clipped differences need not telescope, but this alone is not an implementation bug without matching the intended objective.
\ledger{26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, line 137.

SAMA still supplied no inspectable training implementation.
Further searches for MARS-RA, MARS-Bench, and the primary paper page still did not locate the target trainer.
The generic realized-potential-reuse fixture detects inconsistency when adjacent transitions resample a shared endpoint; source inspection found reused potential tensors in PSPO and V-GEPF and caching in Reward-Shaping-LLM.
This does not certify provider determinism.
The complete mixed outcomes are preserved in
\url{ada/conformance_results.json} and
\url{ada/conformance_suite_02.log}.
\ledger{21, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 139--141.

\paragraph{Additional outside sources and novelty objections.}
The third round adds two particularly relevant source checks.
Schulman et al., \emph{High-Dimensional Continuous Control Using Generalized Advantage Estimation},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02438},
already interprets GAE as more steeply discounting a potential-shaped reward and explains the bias associated with \(\lambda<1\).
The checked pooled LaTeX is
\url{sources/1506.02438.tex}, lines 516--558.
Thus the GAE identity and the generic observation that shaping changes bootstrapping are not new.
\ledger{17, 26}

M\"uller and Kudenko,
\emph{Automating Potential-based Reward Shaping with Vision Language Model Guidance},
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27180},
already learns bounded, terminal-zero potentials from pairwise VLM preferences and varies synthetic oracle-label accuracy.
The pooled LaTeX at
\url{sources/2606.27180.tex}, lines 340--380 and 590--625, supplies the construction and ablation.
A generic comparison-accuracy-versus-learning-speed experiment is therefore already covered.
Together with the previously checked work on initialization and potential offsets, this confines the possible contribution to a sharper attribution protocol and consequential implementation evidence.
\ledger{20, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 143--151.

The additional novelty searches were bounded.
Failure to locate a matching protocol does not establish its absence.
All paper-source checks reported here use the shared-pool records described in the notes.

\paragraph{Disposition of the third-round objections.}
The verifier's scale objection was accepted: the striking V-GEPF root figures came from synthetic \(T=3\) traces, and propagation to the root is negligible at the supplied long rollout lengths.
The source defect remains, but its practical significance is unmeasured and downgraded.
The configuration error was corrected.
Score-induced GAE cuts were separated from true terminal boundaries, preventing an unjustified recommendation to zero every score.
\ledger{17, 19, 23, 26}

The objection that the earlier toy had no exploration problem was addressed by a neural sparse-reward task with rare initial success.
That task demonstrates large intermediate-budget gains under correct accounting and exact shifted-head controls, so large gains alone do not implicate objective distortion.
It remains deliberately simple, with no physical coordination and no actual LMM.
It cannot explain P's particular benchmark improvements.
\ledger{17, 24, 25, 26}

The expanded code audit supplies an additional consumed terminal-residual example in PSPO and retains clean and qualified cases.
It does not provide a prevalence estimate, a benchmark consequence, or evidence that the inspected commits match reported experiments.
No MARS-RA defect has been established.
The verifier's concern about publication readiness remained unresolved.
\ledger{17, 21, 26, 28}

\paragraph{The preceding assessment and proposed next step.}
At the end of the third round, the strongest supported package was an attribution and conformance study:
a conditional mask defect and repair;
a valid objection to P's stated unregularized estimator guarantee;
a two-agent reduction to empirical win shares;
clean return-interface tests;
source-level mismatches in adjacent code;
and neural controls separating objective neutrality from finite-training effects.
The underlying mathematics was established or elementary, and comparison-accuracy ablations already existed.
Benchmark consequences and target-trainer attribution were absent.
\ledger{25, 26}

Q's useful contribution remained the demand for a declared interface and benchmark at each stage.
P's BT estimation error, normalized potential error, GAE discrepancy, and finite-training policy return were different quantities.
Neither estimator consistency nor PBRS neutrality supplied a finite-training consistency--robustness frontier for MAPPO.
There was also a benchmark mismatch: Q's multiplicative minimization convention needs a positive optimum, whereas P uses potentially negative returns; in the neural diagnostic, an optimal cue-following policy has zero failure cost.
An additive performance or sample-complexity comparison required a separately declared model.
\ledger{6, 15, 26}
See \url{ada/round3_attribution.md}, lines 7--13 and 178--186.

The proposed next step was fixed-comparison replay through an available target trainer, preferably MARS-RA:
inspect one exit/reentry trace and one terminal or truncation boundary;
record identities, potentials, rewards, discounts, continuation masks, actor masks, and bootstrap values;
check the return-offset identity on the return propagated to the preceding decision;
and compare two-agent BT potentials with empirical win shares.
Then hold trajectories and comparisons fixed while changing only established accounting defects and matched critic parameterization, followed by a short paired continuation.
A clean result was to be retained.
The long-rollout checks made a football rerun a weaker immediate priority than a fixed-data attribution check.
\ledger{5, 8, 13, 17, 19, 23, 26}

That proposal remains useful, but MARS-RA code was still unlocated.
Verifier entry 28 instead identified a question answerable with the existing diagnostic: whether the blinded-critic failure reflects a changed expected update rather than merely slow learning.
The following additions answer that bounded request without upgrading the preceding implementation findings.
\ledger{28, 29}

\paragraph{Round-four addition: objective invariance versus learner invariance.}
The new question is whether a correctly accounted, fixed potential can change the stationary policy selected by a specified approximate actor--critic update.
The answer is yes in the exactly solvable model below.
This is not a new general observation about function approximation.
The relevant record is
\url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 5--13.
Here \(\lambda\) denotes the GAE trace parameter, not Q's ex ante trust parameter.
It changes the unshaped estimator as well as shaping's effect.
Neither \(\lambda\) nor \(\rho\) automatically becomes a certified consistency--robustness control.
\ledger{29, 31, 33, 44}

\paragraph{The expected update and its residual.}
Assume bounded causal potentials, coherent endpoint reuse, matching discounts, and zero terminal potential and value.
For a fixed policy, use a Markov sufficient state or full history and let \(V^\pi\) be the true unshaped value.
To avoid confusing critic error with the earlier accounting defect, denote the residual in original-value coordinates by
\[
u_t=\widehat V_t^\Phi+\rho\Phi_t-V_t^\pi.
\]
Substitution gives
\[
\delta_t^{\mathrm{sh}}
=\delta_t^{V^\pi}+\gamma u_{t+1}-u_t.
\]
Define the future residual term
\[
B_u(s,a)
=\gamma(1-\lambda)
\sum_{\ell\geq0}(\gamma\lambda)^\ell
\mathbb E[u_{t+\ell+1}\mid s_t=s,a_t=a],
\]
where finite episodes stop at their true terminal state.
Then
\[
\mathbb E[\widehat A_t^{\mathrm{sh}}\mid s,a]
=A^\pi(s,a)-u(s)+B_u(s,a).
\]
The current residual is a pre-action baseline.
The future residual can correlate with the current action.
Under the usual discounted state weighting, the raw expected local actor update is the true policy gradient plus
\[
\frac{1}{1-\gamma}
\mathbb E_{s\sim d^\pi,\,a\sim\pi}
\left[
\nabla_\theta\log\pi_\theta(a\mid s)\,B_u(s,a)
\right].
\]
This is the local PPO gradient at the old policy, where clipping is inactive, subject to the trainer's time weighting.
It is not a convergence theorem for multiple clipped epochs, finite batches, normalization, or shared neural parameters.
Uniform episodic time sampling need not equal the discounted weighting displayed here.
\ledger{31, 33, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 15--50.

To compare shaping with the same approximate unshaped learner, replace \(u\) in the update difference by
\[
h=\widehat V^\Phi+\rho\Phi-\widehat V^0.
\]
This subtracts approximation bias already present at \(\rho=0\).
At \(\lambda=1\), the future residual vanishes from the raw expected update, but finite-sample variance and nonlinear normalization effects remain.
\ledger{30, 31, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 52--68.

\paragraph{A correction to the variance-only interpretation.}
An exact shifted critic makes \(h=0\) pathwise.
Representational capacity alone does not establish that optimization finds that shift.
For fixed critics, fresh potential noise has no direct raw expected actor contribution under a sufficient condition: it has zero mean conditional on the full pre-action history or state, the actor does not consume the realization, and future innovations satisfy the corresponding conditional property.
This does not establish invariance of finite-batch standardization or noisy critic fitting.

Zero mean conditional only on the critic's restricted input is insufficient.
A residual can be invisible to that critic but correlated with a preceding action.
The aliased-branch example below has precisely this property.
The earlier fair-random neural comparator also has an activity-dependent mean that the blind critic cannot necessarily represent, since it observes only its own activity.
Uniform and informative two-query controls were therefore added.
\ledger{32, 35, 40, 43}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 60--68 and 306--308.

\paragraph{Projected-critic compatibility.}
A narrower calculation makes the representation dependence explicit.
Consider a finite discounted Markov model with transition matrix \(P\), expected reward vector \(r\), and \(0<\gamma<1\).
Let \(\Pi\) average within fixed state groups using positive weights for the current policy.
It is linear and nonexpansive in the sup norm.
Assume the critic is at the expected stop-gradient lambda-return regression fixed point.
These assumptions do not automatically hold for general neural regression.

Define
\[
\begin{aligned}
C_\lambda
&=\gamma(1-\lambda)(I-\gamma\lambda P)^{-1}P,\\
T_\lambda v
&=(I-\gamma\lambda P)^{-1}r+C_\lambda v.
\end{aligned}
\]
With shaped reward
\[
r_\Phi=r+\rho(\gamma P-I)\Phi,
\]
the Bellman shift identity is
\[
T_\lambda^\Phi v
=T_\lambda(v+\rho\Phi)-\rho\Phi.
\]
Subtracting the projected fixed-point equations yields
\[
\begin{aligned}
h&=v_\Phi+\rho\Phi-v_0\\
 &=\rho(I-\Pi C_\lambda)^{-1}(I-\Pi)\Phi.
\end{aligned}
\]
Writing
\[
\alpha_\lambda
=\frac{\gamma(1-\lambda)}{1-\gamma\lambda}<1,
\]
the resolvent bound gives
\[
\|h\|_\infty
\leq
\rho\,\frac{1-\gamma\lambda}{1-\gamma}
\|(I-\Pi)\Phi\|_\infty.
\]
Thus a representable potential has zero projected-coordinate discrepancy.
An unrepresentable residual matters to the actor only through its action correlation.
At \(\lambda=1\), \(h=\rho(I-\Pi)\Phi\) can remain nonzero although its future actor-bias coefficient is zero.
This is elementary projected Bellman algebra, not a new general theorem.
\ledger{33, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 70--109.

The checks in
\url{ada/round4_exact.py}
cover 576 rational two-step cases and 720 random matrix instances.
The maximum matrix-identity error is below \(7.1\times10^{-14}\).
These checks test the implementation of the formulas, not their applicability to P.
The record is
\url{ada/round4_exact_01.log}.
\ledger{44}

\paragraph{An exactly solvable harmful update.}
A root action \(a\in\{0,1\}\) selects a branch.
At the next step the episode gives reward \(R_a\) and terminates; root environmental reward is zero.
The actor chooses action one with probability \(p=\sigma(\theta)\).
The critic sees time but cannot distinguish branch identity.
Potentials are zero at root and terminal and equal \(\phi_a\) at the branch.
The shaped rewards are
\[
\gamma\rho\phi_a,
\qquad
R_a-\rho\phi_a,
\]
whose discounted sum is exactly \(\gamma R_a\).

Write
\[
\overline R=(1-p)R_0+pR_1,
\qquad
\overline\phi=(1-p)\phi_0+p\phi_1.
\]
At the projected critic fixed point,
\[
v_1=\overline R-\rho\overline\phi,
\qquad
v_0=\gamma\overline R.
\]
The root GAE is
\[
\widehat A_0(a)
=\gamma\left[
\lambda(R_a-\overline R)
+\rho(1-\lambda)(\phi_a-\overline\phi)
\right].
\]
For
\[
\Delta R=R_1-R_0>0,
\qquad
\Delta\phi=\phi_1-\phi_0,
\]
define
\[
D=\lambda\Delta R+\rho(1-\lambda)\Delta\phi.
\]
The expected root logit update is
\[
\dot\theta=\gamma p(1-p)D.
\]
Both critic parameters contribute only action-independent terms, so this raw actor field also holds before critic convergence.
The sign change is not caused by critic lag.
\ledger{31, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 111--147.

Without entropy, finite initial logits tend toward the better action if \(D>0\), the worse action if \(D<0\), and remain unchanged if \(D=0\).
The deterministic limits are boundary policies, not finite softmax logits.
The same unshaped aliased learner has \(D=\lambda\Delta R>0\) for every \(\lambda>0\).
At \(\lambda=0\), its signal vanishes, so that degenerate baseline cannot be counted as a shaping-induced failure.

Reversed potentials select the worse action when
\[
\rho(1-\lambda)|\Delta\phi|>\lambda\Delta R.
\]
The relevant relative coefficient is therefore
\[
\frac{\rho(1-\lambda)}{\lambda},
\]
together with reward and potential gaps, rather than a universal product \(\rho(1-\lambda)\).
For \(\Delta R=0.04\), \(\Delta\phi=-1\), and \(\rho=1\), the threshold is \(\lambda=1/1.04\).
Strict reversal also occurs with interior potentials:
\[
\Delta R=0.02,\quad
\phi_0=0.9,\quad
\phi_1=0.1,\quad
\lambda=0.95,\quad
\rho=1.
\]
Thus infinite BT logits are unnecessary.
\ledger{31, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 149--158.

A second forced-action agent with complementary potential embeds the example in a cooperative per-agent shaping model.
It supplies no actor gradient that cancels the acting agent's bias.
No turnover or accounting defect is needed.
A branch-aware critic representing the true shaped value restores action gap \(\gamma\Delta R\).
An analytic shifted head on the old aliased critic instead restores its original gap \(\gamma\lambda\Delta R\).
Repairing task-value approximation and reproducing the baseline learner are different interventions.
\ledger{31, 44}

\paragraph{Joint equilibria, entropy, and normalization.}
With Shannon entropy coefficient \(\tau>0\) and no normalization, the actor field becomes
\[
\dot\theta=p(1-p)(\gamma D-\tau\theta).
\]
Its stable finite-logit equilibrium is
\[
p_\Phi=\sigma(\gamma D/\tau),
\qquad
p_0=\sigma(\gamma\lambda\Delta R/\tau).
\]
The expected semi-gradient critic dynamics, omitting positive learning-rate factors, are
\[
\begin{aligned}
\dot v_1
&=\overline R-\rho\overline\phi-v_1,\\
\dot v_0
&=\gamma\left[
\lambda\overline R+
(1-\lambda)(\rho\overline\phi+v_1)
\right]-v_0.
\end{aligned}
\]
For a fixed actor these form stable triangular linear dynamics.
The actor field is independent of critic parameters; at the finite entropy equilibrium its derivative is \(-\tau p(1-p)<0\).
The joint Jacobian is therefore block triangular with negative eigenvalues.
This establishes a locally stable joint equilibrium in the declared expected-dynamics model, not merely a field evaluated at an arbitrary critic.

For
\[
\Delta R=0.04,\quad
\Delta\phi=-1,\quad
\gamma=0.99,\quad
\lambda=0.95,\quad
\rho=1,\quad
\tau=0.02,
\]
the better-action probability falls from \(0.867726\) unshaped to \(0.355718\) shaped.
The 224 expected-dynamics runs in
\url{ada/round4_exact_results.json}
check entropy and normalization variants.
Finite integration endpoints are not labeled exact equilibria when their residual update has not vanished.
\ledger{31, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 160--187.

For population normalization over root action samples only,
\[
s(p)=\gamma|D|\sqrt{p(1-p)}.
\]
With stabilizer \(\varepsilon_n>0\),
\[
\dot\theta
=p(1-p)
\left[
\frac{\gamma D}{s(p)+\varepsilon_n}
-\tau\theta
\right].
\]
Without entropy, normalization preserves the sign threshold.
With fixed entropy coefficient, it changes the relative strength of the task update.
In the formal zero-stabilizer limit, the magnitude of nonzero \(D\) cancels.
Finite-batch normalization of noisy advantages can introduce further effects through its random denominator.
These conclusions are specific to the stated normalization model.
\ledger{37, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 189--197.

\paragraph{Finite queries can change normalized equilibria.}
At branch \(a\), draw \(K\) independent judgments with preference probability \(q_a\), use their empirical win share as the potential, and reuse it in both adjacent differences.
Returns still telescope exactly.
Let
\[
b=\rho(1-\lambda),
\qquad
D=\lambda\Delta R+b(q_1-q_0).
\]
The root advantage variance is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Var}(\widehat A_0)
=\gamma^2\Bigg[
&D^2p(1-p)\\
&+\frac{b^2}{K}
\big\{(1-p)q_0(1-q_0)+pq_1(1-q_1)\big\}
\Bigg].
\end{aligned}
\]
Query count changes this variance but not the raw expected actor field.
Population advantage normalization with a fixed entropy coefficient converts that variance change into a changed stationary policy.

For the deliberately misaligned comparator
\[
q_0=0.9,\quad q_1=0.1,\quad
\Delta R=0.04,\quad \gamma=0.99,
\]
with \(\lambda=0.95\), \(\rho=1\), and \(\tau=0.1\), the better-action equilibrium probabilities are approximately
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}
K&1&2&4&8&16\\
\hline
p_\Phi&0.209&0.132&0.0653&0.0226&0.00482
\end{array}
\]
The root and derivative checks establish unique stable roots for these parameter choices.
This is increasingly precise estimation of the same wrong latent preference, not improving accuracy about the correct task target and not a result about P's experiments.
\ledger{37, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 199--214, and
\url{ada/round4_query_noise_01.log}.

A population-level control divides both the raw reward gradient and an entropy term with fixed unnormalized coefficient by the same standard deviation.
Equivalently, after standardizing advantages, use entropy coefficient
\[
\frac{\tau_0}{s+\varepsilon_n}.
\]
The field is then a positive scalar multiple of the unnormalized field, restoring its fixed points in this example.
It removes the normalization/entropy confound, not the misaligned-comparator bias.
It does not establish identical finite-batch PPO trajectories.
\ledger{44}

\paragraph{What conditional performance bounds follow.}
A generic residual-to-value bound requires a policy-improvement premise.
In a finite fully observed discounted MDP, suppose a policy satisfies, at every state,
\[
\max_a
\left[
A^\pi(s,a)+B_u(s,a)
-\mathbb E_{a'\sim\pi}B_u(s,a')
\right]
\leq\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt}}.
\]
This statewise approximate-greediness condition does not follow from a small arbitrary neural gradient.
If \(\|u\|_\infty\leq E\), the action oscillation of \(B_u\) is at most \(2\alpha_\lambda E\).
The performance-difference lemma gives
\[
J^\star-J(\pi)
\leq
\frac{\epsilon_{\mathrm{opt}}+2\alpha_\lambda E}{1-\gamma}.
\]
The projection calculation bounds additional shaping discrepancy, but the unshaped critic's approximation error is still needed for this value bound.
This argument overlaps established biased-advantage analysis.
An oracle assumption \(\rho\Phi^\star=V^\pi\) would make the shaped true value zero and permit an error-sensitive specialization, but that policy-dependent value target is not P's relative-contribution or Shapley target.
\ledger{33, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 216--240.

The two-step family admits a more concrete additive envelope.
Assume \(R_a,\widehat\phi_a\in[0,1]\), \(\lambda>0\), no entropy, and the exact expected-update limit.
Declare the task-aligned target \(\phi_a^\star=R_a\) and
\[
\eta=\max_a|\widehat\phi_a-R_a|,
\qquad b=\rho(1-\lambda).
\]
Then
\[
J^\star-J(\pi_\infty)
\leq
\gamma\min\left\{
1,\frac b\lambda,\frac{2b\eta}{\lambda+b}
\right\}.
\]
Indeed, a wrong limiting action requires \(D\leq0\).
Arbitrary bounded predictions imply \(\lambda\Delta R\leq b\).
Writing
\[
\Delta\widehat\phi
=\Delta R+\delta_1-\delta_0,
\qquad
|\delta_1-\delta_0|\leq2\eta,
\]
gives \((\lambda+b)\Delta R\leq2b\eta\).
Ties also satisfy the envelope.
Exact predictions give zero loss; reversed extreme potentials approach the arbitrary-prediction threshold.
The recorded random-parameter check covers 100,000 cases in
\url{ada/round4_bound_check_01.log}.

This is a restricted additive consistency/error/robustness statement, not a finite-sample or MARS-RA guarantee.
The unshaped expected learner is already asymptotically optimal for every positive \(\lambda\), so shaping offers no asymptotic consistency improvement to trade for robustness in this family.
\ledger{44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 242--257.

\paragraph{Horizon prevents a universal trust coefficient.}
Delay the same branch reward to transition \(H-1\), keep branch potential \(\phi_a\) at every intermediate state, and let the critic see time but not branch identity.
The root GAE action gap at the projected fixed point is
\[
\begin{aligned}
D_H={}&(\gamma\lambda)^{H-1}\Delta R\\
&+\rho\gamma(1-\lambda)
\sum_{\ell=0}^{H-2}(\gamma\lambda)^\ell\Delta\phi.
\end{aligned}
\]
The true return gap remains \(\gamma^{H-1}\Delta R\).
For finite-horizon \(\gamma=1\),
\[
D_H
=\lambda^{H-1}\Delta R
+\rho(1-\lambda^{H-1})\Delta\phi.
\]
For every \(\rho>0\) and \(0<\lambda<1\), a sufficiently long finite delay makes reversed bounded guidance select the worse action even at \(\Delta R=1\).
The identical unshaped expected learner retains positive drift toward the better action.
Consequently, no nontrivial loss bound uniform over all finite horizons follows from a small product \(\rho(1-\lambda)\) alone.

At \(H=24\), \(\gamma=0.99\), \(\lambda=0.95\), and \(\rho=1\), rewards \(-1,1\) and reversed unit-range potentials already reverse the GAE gap.
This separate delayed-choice example shares the neural task's horizon and reward scale but is not that simulator.
The rational checks cover 450 cases in
\url{ada/round4_delayed_01.log}.
The mechanism is an explicit application of established GAE horizon bias.
\ledger{39, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 259--280.

\paragraph{Longer neural controls.}
The fourth round retains the run-of-six task, horizon 24, sparse rewards, turnover, networks, optimizers, and coherent global-time accounting.
It adds normalization switches, potential inputs to the blind critic, and shifted-head controls.
Long conditions use 1,600 updates and 32 seeds each.
The sensitivity matrix uses 320 updates and 32 seeds per condition, with
\[
\lambda\in\{0,0.5,0.8,0.9,0.95,0.99,1\},
\qquad
\rho\in\{0.25,1,4\},
\]
plus matched unshaped baselines.
The matrix is not a complete factorial design.
Uniform and informative two-query long controls separate fresh sampling noise from the activity-dependent mean.
This remains a custom diagnostic, not XuanCe, an established external MARL benchmark, or a MARS-RA replication.
\ledger{34, 35, 38, 40, 43}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 298--308.

The completed long results are as follows.
All shaped conditions use unit strength.
Uncertainty is standard error across seed-level evaluations; each evaluation uses 2,048 fresh episodes.
\[
\begin{array}{lcr}
\text{Condition}&\lambda&\text{Final success}\\
\hline
\text{Unshaped, normalized}&0.95&0.999664\pm0.000156\\
\text{Exact, normalized}&0.95&1.000000\pm0.000000\\
\text{Reversed, normalized}&0.95&0.000000\pm0.000000\\
\text{Fair random, normalized}&0.95&0.997711\pm0.000568\\
\text{Unshaped, normalized}&1&0.998764\pm0.000288\\
\text{Reversed, normalized}&1&0.997513\pm0.001256\\
\text{Unshaped, unnormalized}&0.95&0.994781\pm0.000429\\
\text{Reversed, unnormalized}&0.95&0.000000\pm0.000000\\
\text{Reversed, shifted head}&0.95&0.999695\pm0.000075\\
\text{Reversed, potential input}&0.95&0.813614\pm0.057986
\end{array}
\]
All conditions other than the reversed blind conditions and potential-input condition have 32 of 32 seeds above \(0.9\) success.
The reversed blind conditions have none.
The potential-input condition has 24 of 32; its remaining eight seeds lie between \(0.24365\) and \(0.26953\), while its median is one.
A mean alone conceals this split.
An empirical zero is not a proof of exactly zero true success probability.
\ledger{34, 38, 40, 43}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 328--349.

During updates 81--160, mean training success is
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Unshaped}:&\quad 0.43896\pm0.04843,\\
\text{Exact}:&\quad 0.73863\pm0.04773,\\
\text{Fair random}:&\quad 0.27057\pm0.02152.
\end{aligned}
\]
All later reach near-perfect success, while reversed guidance remains unsuccessful at the long budget.
Normalization is therefore not necessary for the reversed-guidance failure.
These are persistent finite-budget observations, not a proof of neural asymptotic failure.
\ledger{38, 41, 43}

The uniform long control reaches \(0.9989\pm0.0004\), and informative two-query guidance reaches one in all 32 runs.
Their paired differences from the unshaped long baseline are
\[
-0.000809\pm0.000381,
\qquad
0.000336\pm0.000156,
\]
respectively.
Fair-random minus uniform final success is
\[
-0.001144\pm0.000625.
\]
The informative two-query versus exact early-window paired difference is \(0.00580\pm0.03288\).
Thus this diagnostic does not establish a clear speed advantage of exact over two-query predictions.
This is not an equivalence claim and does not contradict measurements on P's different benchmarks.
\ledger{35, 43, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 351--378.

\paragraph{Sensitivity and the limits of the long-run conclusion.}
At 320 updates, the mean-success sensitivity matrix is
\[
\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
&\text{Unshaped}&\rho=0.25&\rho=1&\rho=4\\
\hline
\lambda=0&0.0636&0&0&0\\
0.5&0.5005&0&0&0\\
0.8&0.6504&0&0&0\\
0.9&0.8107&0.0214&0&0\\
0.95&0.9108&0.2769&0&0\\
0.99&0.9239&0.9031&0.6896&0.0556\\
1&0.9500&0.9172&0.8593&0.6509
\end{array}
\]
The shaped columns use reversed guidance.
All standard errors, seed values, and success counts are retained in
\url{ada/round4_summary_final_01.json}
and
\url{ada/round4_summary_final_01.csv}.
For example, at \(\lambda=0.99\), the unit-strength result is \(0.6896\pm0.0630\), strength four gives \(0.0556\pm0.0153\), and the baseline is \(0.9239\pm0.0391\).
This supports a graded interaction rather than a universal cutoff.

At \(\lambda=1\), strength four still gives \(0.6509\pm0.0651\) versus \(0.9500\pm0.0325\) unshaped.
That finite-training harm cannot be attributed to the future projected-GAE bias term, which vanishes at \(\lambda=1\).
Unit-strength reversed guidance improves from about \(0.859\) at 320 updates to \(0.9975\) at 1,600.
No corresponding long run was performed for strength four or every previously harmful exact-guidance condition.
The evidence does not classify all harms as either transient or permanent.
\ledger{30, 43, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 355--380.

\paragraph{Exact frozen-policy checks in the neural task.}
Finite-state dynamic programming separates sampling noise and critic lag from the information restriction.
The analysis enumerates progress, cooldown, cue, and time, and computes occupancy, value, GAE moments, and actor score updates.
A projected table critic has the same information as the blind neural critic: time, own activity, and identity.
It removes fitting error within that information class.
The calculation compares uniform actor-slot weighting with discounted-objective weighting and also computes population normalization and entropy.
These are local expected fields, not the full multi-epoch PPO map.
\ledger{36, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 310--316.

The initial failed-policy calculation suffered catastrophic cancellation around a constant negative reward.
Its gradient cosines are invalid and remain preserved as diagnostic history in
\url{ada/round4_dp_reversed_01.json}.
The corrected calculation removes the action-independent terminal baseline analytically.
Final outputs are
\url{ada/round4_dp_reversed_03.json}
and
\url{ada/round4_dp_unshaped_02.json}.
Probability conservation, the random-policy success probability \(0.0152964502\), and directional finite differences pass.
The worst relative finite-difference discrepancy is \(1.46\times10^{-5}\), on the extremely small failed-policy objective.
\ledger{36, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 318--320, and
\url{ada/check_round4_dp_01.log}.

At the failed actor, exact success is \(1.1588\times10^{-20}\).
The projected reversed-advice raw actor field has norm \(4.3260\times10^{-4}\) and cosine \(-0.5057\) with the discounted task gradient.
The learned critic produces essentially the same field.
The projected unshaped field instead has norm \(2.8731\times10^{-20}\) and positive alignment.
Better fitting within the blind information class therefore does not remove the harmful raw direction.

With population normalization and entropy coefficient \(0.01\), the remaining learned-critic field has norm \(1.9390\times10^{-4}\), about \(3.96\%\) of the sum of opposing reward and entropy gradient norms.
It is near cancellation, not zero, and its remaining direction has positive task-gradient alignment.
No neural stationary-point certificate follows.
\ledger{36, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 322--324.

At the successful frozen actor, exact success is \(0.99998846\).
Introducing reversed advice with the projected blind critic produces raw field norm \(0.03894\) and cosine \(-0.9460\) against the discounted task gradient.
Population normalization plus entropy keeps the direction harmful, with cosine about \(-0.9480\).
The unshaped field remains aligned.
This demonstrates an adverse local update away from a successful policy without relying on the failed policy's vanishing task signal.
At \(\lambda=1\), the raw expected task-gradient contribution is invariant as the algebra predicts.
\ledger{36, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, line 326.

\paragraph{Numerical equivalence and verification.}
The analytic shifted-head implementation rearranges algebraically canceling floating-point operations.
Its traces agree through the original 160-update window, but some 1,600-update traces diverge after approximately 200--600 updates.
This qualifies any claim of arbitrarily long bitwise agreement without changing the exact-arithmetic identity.

The canonical control in
\url{ada/round4_canonical.py}
performs training directly in original-value coordinates.
All eight additional canonical runs reproduce the corresponding unshaped success observations and final evaluations across 1,600 updates.
The stronger recorded check also matches advantage standard deviation and value loss.
These are equivalence checks, not new independent effect-estimation seeds.
\ledger{38, 40, 43}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, line 351, and
\url{ada/round4_canonical_check_02.log}.

The fourth round completed 1,216 main trials and 64 extra controls, all declared conditions at 32 seeds.
The verification checks configuration, seed, horizon, update count, finite diagnostics, per-trial logs, and return/GAE identities.
Maximum errors are
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{GAE identity}:&\quad 5.9952\times10^{-15},\\
\text{Return telescoping}:&\quad 7.1054\times10^{-15}.
\end{aligned}
\]
The record is
\url{ada/round4_verify_01.log};
versions and hashes are in
\url{ada/round4_manifest_01.json}.
Checkpoint reproductions and canonical checks are not counted as additional independent statistical seeds.
Incomplete snapshots and invalid initial numerical outputs are retained with their qualifications; the complete summary supersedes interim summaries.
\ledger{43, 44}
See \url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 398--402.

\paragraph{Fourth-round outside sources and novelty limits.}
The additional primary sources substantially narrow the contribution.
\ledger{33, 44}
\begin{itemize}
\item Schulman et al.,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02438},
already gives the shaped-reward interpretation of GAE and its effective-horizon bias.
The new residual derivation specializes that mechanism.
\item Bertsekas,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.02426},
explicitly distinguishes invariance of exact optimal policies from changes in suboptimal policies produced by approximate TD, SARSA, and aggregation.
The checked pooled text is
\url{sources/1910.02426.txt}, lines 180--200.
\item Li, Xie, and Lu, \emph{Difference Advantage Estimation for Multi-Agent Policy Gradients},
\url{https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/li22w.html},
Theorem A.6 and Corollary A.7, already connects credit assignment, biased advantages, stationary policies, and performance-difference bounds.
\item Xiao, Ramasubramanian, and Poovendran,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.15941},
bounds the original-game policy-gradient norm at a shaped equilibrium using critic approximation error and a score-function bound.
The inspected source passage is lines 460--511.
This is close prior art for approximation-dependent stationarity, not a theorem that shaped and unshaped equilibrium policies coincide.
\item Lo et al.,
\url{https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v25/24-0040.html},
Appendix C.1, states linear-approximation invariance when the potential belongs to the same feature class.
Earlier pooled versions
\url{sources/2206.02902.tex}
and
\url{sources/2406.01562.tex}
must not be described as containing that exact final appendix.
\item M\"uller and Kudenko,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.01307}
and
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27180},
already study initialization, offsets, and noisy VLM preference potentials.
A counterexample to an unrestricted claim about an approximate learner does not invalidate objective-invariance proofs.
\item Bahrpeyma,
\url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.08158},
also discusses limits of applying invariance theorems to approximate deep-RL optimization.
The inspected source locations are lines 353, 393, 478, and 1374--1376.
\end{itemize}

The source and search record is
\url{ada/round4_algorithmic_invariance.md}, lines 282--296.
Queries targeted PBRS, GAE, function approximation, projection, actor--critic bias, and suboptimal policies.
Failed searches are not novelty evidence.
Pool resolution for DAE and initially for \emph{Bootstrapped Reward Shaping} encountered Semantic Scholar HTTP 429 errors.
DAE was checked through its publisher page and web-accessible text without saving a separate agent-folder paper copy.
\emph{Bootstrapped Reward Shaping} was subsequently pooled as
\url{sources/2501.00989.tex}; its main contraction argument concerns an evolving value-derived potential rather than this fixed-potential projection example.
No new paper retrieval was needed for the present consolidation.
\ledger{33, 44}

\paragraph{Resolution of review 28.}
Entry 28 asked whether the reversed blind-critic failure could be a changed update rather than a slow transient.
The exact aliased-critic construction answers that question positively within its model, including joint actor/critic stability, entropy, normalization, and comparison with the identical unshaped learner.
The horizon extension shows why a universal robustness coefficient based only on \(\rho(1-\lambda)\) is insufficient.
The restricted additive envelope makes the benchmark explicit but offers no asymptotic improvement over its already optimal unshaped baseline.
\ledger{28, 31, 37, 39, 44}

The longer neural experiments show that reversed guidance remains harmful through ten times the original budget, with and without normalization.
Fresh fair noise, uniform guidance, and informative two-query guidance recover near-perfect success.
The projected finite-state calculation corroborates a harmful local field that persists after removing critic fitting error.
However, the neural entropy-adjusted field is not certified stationary, and no infinite-time PPO failure is proved.
The earlier claim that the failure was merely transient is withdrawn; the opposite unrestricted asymptotic claim is also unsupported.
\ledger{30, 34, 35, 36, 38, 40, 43, 44}

The review's request to distinguish mean-zero noise from hidden information required a correction: conditional mean zero given the critic input is too weak.
The analysis now uses full pre-action conditioning for a variance-only sufficient condition and explicitly separates activity-dependent mean potentials from sampling innovations.
The optional external MARL benchmark was not run in round four.
No actual MARS-RA critic restriction, implementation defect, or benchmark consequence was established.
\ledger{28, 32, 35, 44}

\paragraph{What remained open after round four.}
The original proposal to relabel P's estimator result as a Q-style algorithmic guarantee should be rejected as a publication claim.
The supported replacement is an attribution study of learned potential shaping under approximate critics.
Correct accounting, potential-estimation quality, critic compatibility, action correlation, horizon, query noise, advantage normalization, and entropy scale are distinct parts of that interface.
A scalar comparator-accuracy rate does not characterize them.
\ledger{42, 44}

The broad objective-versus-approximation distinction and the principal analytical tools are already established.
The projection identity, explicit threshold, delayed-choice example, and additive toy envelope are useful explanatory calculations, not sufficient evidence of a new theoretical contribution.
The shifted-head control removes helpful shaping effects as well as harmful ones; it is a diagnostic that reproduces a baseline, not a new algorithm guaranteeing improvement.
\ledger{33, 41, 44}

P's permanent-coordinate alignment, singleton behavior, estimator regularization, potential reuse, reward and GAE masks, bootstrap handling, and critic access to comparison information remain unknown.
At the end of round four, no MARS-RA training implementation had been located, no standard external MARL benchmark had been tested, and the inspected adjacent-code commits had not been linked conclusively to published experiments.
The source audit remains a convenience sample with synthetic-input findings and no measured benchmark consequence.
The full strength sweep was not extended to 1,600 updates, and the long lambda-one control does not resolve every earlier short-budget harm.
These limitations left the publication case thin.
\ledger{26, 28, 43, 44}

\paragraph{The next step proposed after round four.}
The next discriminating question was whether measured critic-coordinate mismatch explains predictor-quality or query-budget effects in an actual learned-shaping trainer after accounting and optimizer-scale controls.
The proposed test uses an available target implementation, preferably MARS-RA if obtained, and begins with a frozen checkpoint and a small fixed set of trajectories and stored comparison outputs.
First apply the existing boundary and return-offset checks, retaining clean outcomes.
For two agents, verify whether the implemented potential agrees with empirical win shares under its stated tie and regularization conventions.
\ledger{13, 26, 44}

On those same data, compare raw and normalized actor updates under the original critic, a matched shifted critic, and an appropriately fitted critic with the necessary potential information.
Keep entropy scaling explicit and reuse the same potential realizations.
Where feasible, estimate task-gradient alignment or an independent task-return directional effect.
Then perform a short paired continuation from the same checkpoint and evaluate the unshaped objective.
This tests whether the diagnosed update difference predicts an actual training consequence without new LMM calls.
\ledger{36, 37, 44}

A consequential result on a target trainer or standard benchmark could support an empirical attribution paper.
A clean or negligible result would leave the existing cautions and exact examples intact while weakening that publication route.
Neither outcome would establish prevalence or a general consistency--robustness frontier.
Entry 45 marked readiness to consolidate this limited result, not publication readiness.
\ledger{44, 45}

\paragraph{Round-five addition: the latest review and transfer question.}
The latest verifier review is entry 46, not entry 28.
It accepted the preceding qualified record but returned \emph{ITERATE}, rating the publication case \([rating withheld]\).
The unresolved test was whether harm survived an ordinary critic restriction, graded label accuracy, and a standard trainer and task.
It explicitly allowed a clean or small result and rejected repeating the same target merely to obtain stronger harm.
Entries 47--60 report the bounded response.
The preceding fourth-round assessments are historical: a standard external environment has now been tested, but MARS-RA itself has not.
\ledger{46, 47, 58, 59, 60}
The complete account is
\url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 3--17 and 100--124.

\paragraph{Standard trainer, task, and objective.}
The experiment executes the unchanged
\texttt{R\_MAPPO}, \texttt{R\_MAPPOPolicy}, actor, critic, and
\texttt{SeparatedReplayBuffer} from the already audited public MAPPO commit.
A rollout adapter supplies rewards and the explicitly named potential-input and shifted-critic controls.
Downloaded-source hashes are checked; no native trainer implementation is replaced.
The environment is MPE2 1.1.1
\texttt{simple\_reference\_v3}, with native discrete actions, observations, dynamics, and distance rewards, using its supported shared-reward setting \texttt{local\_ratio=0}.
Each agent observes its teammate's target, so its own target must be communicated.
A joint-state comparator can use information absent directly from a local observation, although learned communication may reveal it.
\ledger{48, 58}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 11--15.

Separate per-agent actors and critics use the implementation's supported non-sharing configuration.
Local critics receive 21-dimensional native observations; centralized critics receive their 42-dimensional concatenation.
This avoids requiring identical shared critic inputs to predict different per-agent shaped values.
Neither critic receives time explicitly, and neither is certified to represent the exact finite-horizon value.
The networks have two hidden layers of width 64; native clipping, advantage standardization, optimizer, and value-loss machinery remain.
\ledger{48, 49, 58}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 15 and 53.

The declared objective is discounted return over 25 steps, with \(\gamma=0.99\).
The native time-limit truncation is deliberately treated as the terminal boundary of that finite-horizon problem, with zero value and potential.
This is not a continuing-task bootstrap test.
The predeclared primary display metric is native undiscounted episode return; discounted return was also recorded from the start.
PBRS invariance concerns the latter objective.
There is no turnover, and rewards are dense.
Thus this experiment isolates a standard learning interface; it does not reproduce P's sparse embodied benchmark.
\ledger{48, 57, 59}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 17 and 49--53.

\paragraph{Graded labels and reward routing.}
Let \(d_i(s)\) be agent \(i\)'s distance to its own true goal and
\[
g(s)=\mathbf 1\{d_0(s)<d_1(s)\}.
\]
Each independent label equals \(g(s)\) with probability \(a\) and is flipped otherwise.
For \(K\) queries, the potential is
\[
\Phi(s)=(W/K,1-W/K).
\]
Exact distance ties receive \((1/2,1/2)\).
This directly implements the two-agent BT normalized estimate or its boundary limit, without fitting divergent logits.
Accuracy is agreement with the declared distance ordering, not Shapley accuracy or verified semantic contribution.
Away from ties,
\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E[\Phi^0(s)]
 &=\frac12+(2a-1)\left(g(s)-\frac12\right),\\
\operatorname{Var}(\Phi^0(s))
 &=\frac{a(1-a)}K.
\end{aligned}
\]
Accuracy therefore changes the conditional mean's orientation and magnitude; query count changes sampling variance.
This is not a policy-performance reduction.
Independent simulated flips do not establish independence or absence of position bias in actual LMM queries.
No LMM calls or inference-cost benefit are claimed.
\ledger{49, 58, 59}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 19--33.

Every potential realization is stored and reused at both adjacent endpoints.
All agents retain every shaping transition, and the terminal potential is zero.
Initial states and comparison draws are policy independent, so
\[
\sum_{t=0}^{H-1}\gamma^t
(r_t^{\mathrm{sh},i}-r_t)
=-\rho\Phi_0^i
\]
holds trajectory by trajectory.
The identity is checked in float64 before native float32 storage.
\ledger{48, 58}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 35--41.

For this fixed team, the simplex normalization gives
\[
\sum_i\rho F_t^i=
\begin{cases}
\rho(\gamma-1),&t<H-1,\\
-\rho,&t=H-1.
\end{cases}
\]
Averaging the shaped rewards into a common team reward would therefore erase comparator information before return estimation.
The experiment preserves per-agent rewards and separate buffers.
This elementary routing diagnostic neither alleges that P averages its rewards nor supplies a new theorem.
The notation includes \(\rho\) explicitly, correcting entry 53.
\ledger{53, 54, 58}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, line 43.

\paragraph{Declared matrix and attribution controls.}
Before main outcomes, the declaration fixed 42 conditions, eight seeds each, and 160,000 environment steps per trial: 400 updates of 16 complete episodes.
Eight selected long conditions use 800,000 steps, or 2,000 updates.
The selected matrix crosses local and centralized critics with accuracy levels
\[
a\in\{0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1\},
\]
query counts \(K\in\{2,16\}\), GAE parameters \(\lambda\in\{0.95,1\}\), and weights \(\rho\in\{0,1,4\}\).
It is not a complete factorial design.
Controls add potential information, use an analytic shifted head, or remove entropy.
A separately declared supplement adds matching unshaped potential-input critics, before inspecting the shaped input-control outcomes.
This distinguishes additional critic information from a shaping-specific repair.
\ledger{49, 51, 58}
See \url{ada/round5_declaration.json} and
\url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 45--55.

Each final evaluation uses 256 newly seeded stochastic-policy episodes.
Native and discounted returns are retained.
Final distance and the fraction ending with both agents within \(0.25\) of their goals are secondary diagnostics; the latter is not a native success criterion.
Comparisons pair training seeds against matching critic, lambda, and entropy baselines.
Intervals across eight seeds are descriptive and unadjusted for multiple comparisons.
Negative native returns are compared additively, not converted into competitive ratios.
Three pilots remain separate from the main results.
\ledger{49, 58, 59}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 47--53.

Initial, middle, and final checkpoints compare raw and standardized actor estimates with sampled discounted task-return gradients.
Separate frozen-policy diagnostics collect 512 fresh episodes, fit on 384, and evaluate on 128 held-out episodes.
They compare native critics, shaped refits, potential-input refits, and the analytic matched shift, checking the residual-to-GAE identity for
\[
h=\widehat V^{\mathrm{sh}}+\rho\Phi-\widehat V^0.
\]
These finite Monte Carlo fits are not certified projected fixed points.
Missing time information, fitting error, and policy-dependent coverage remain.
Native mismatch also exists without shaping, so its entire magnitude cannot be assigned to shaping.
Sample cosines are not exact population gradients or causal mediation estimates.
\ledger{58, 59}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 57--67.

\paragraph{Short-budget results.}
At 160,000 steps, the local unshaped native return is \(-18.501\).
With unit weight, the paired accuracy-sweep differences are
\[
\begin{array}{cr}
a&\text{Native-return difference}\\
\hline
0&-0.176\pm0.094\\
0.25&-0.032\pm0.070\\
0.5&+0.029\pm0.110\\
0.75&+0.113\pm0.097\\
1&+0.093\pm0.076
\end{array}
\]
Uncertainty is standard error across paired seeds.
These small effects have overlapping uncertainty.
The unit-weight centralized wrong-label difference is \(-0.042\pm0.162\).
\ledger{50, 58}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, line 73, and
\url{ada/round5_summary_final_01.json}.

At weight four, always-wrong labels give native-return differences
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{Local}:&\quad -0.338\pm0.140,\\
\text{Centralized}:&\quad -0.600\pm0.201.
\end{aligned}
\]
Discounted differences are respectively \(-0.296\pm0.121\) and \(-0.525\pm0.174\).
Exact labels at the same weight give \(-0.011\pm0.033\) and \(+0.009\pm0.195\).
Removing entropy leaves wrong-label losses \(-0.529\pm0.137\) locally and \(-0.623\pm0.188\) centrally.
Fixed entropy is therefore not their sole source.
At \(\lambda=1\), weight-four wrong-label differences become \(+0.031\pm0.186\) and \(+0.080\pm0.135\), but the corresponding unshaped baselines themselves perform worse at this budget.
This does not recommend lambda one as an overall improvement.
\ledger{56, 58}
See \url{ada/round5_standard_transfer.md}, lines 73--75.

